a variant of coronavirus first  identified in india has today been  classified  as a variant of concern by the uk  government more than  500 cases of b16172  have now been detected across england  this is a sharp rise from the 202 cases  officially recorded by public health  england on the 28th of april but it is  still  a small proportion of overall cases  the indian variant has two key mutations  in its spike protein the first is called  l452r which has also been found  in a variant circulating in california  although the indian variant has evolved  independently  the second is called e484q  a completely new mutation lab tests  suggest  both help the virus to infect cells and  to evade  antibodies while it's still unknown what  real world effect  that would have on vaccines it's got all  the hallmarks  of a dangerous virus and phe today said  it's at least  as transmissible as the kent variant  well here's the situation with  coronavirus in the uk  at the moment low cases overall but  numbers higher  in places where we know the indian  variant has been located  the first cases were found in northern  ireland for example  the local government region of derry  city and strabane  both above the uk average with a 16  rise in cases over the last week  similarly bolton in the northwest is  also  seeing a spike in the week to the first  of may it had  a total of 241  cases that's a 75 rise  cases of the indian variant are thought  to be found in schools care homes  and places of worship largely linked to  travel  to the subcontinent and despite london  seeing fewer cases  it's still an area of public health  england has identified  as seeing a surge in cases of this  indian variant  phe says there is currently insufficient  evidence to indicate that  any of the indian variants cause more  severe disease or make vaccines  less effective but boris johnson did say  the government needs to think very  carefully  about the public health response
 

